\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Every borrowed result is re-used at a smaller scale than its origin assumed: the adversary is co-located rather than networked, the bonded act is coordination rather than validation, and the record is evidence rather than law. Nothing in the four lineages is discarded --- each is narrowed by one assumption, and the narrowing is what makes the composition deployable on one machine.}
\label{bonded:tab:related-borrow}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2pt}
% Lineage column widened 0.19 -> 0.22\textwidth: "Cryptoeconomic" (14
% letters, no hyphen point TeX would take) was still 8.5pt over at 0.19.
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.22\textwidth} >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Lineage} & \textbf{What we borrow} & \textbf{What we depart from} & \textbf{Why} \\
\midrule
Bonded participation (PoS slashing) & Slash-$>$-deviation-gain soundness argument & Byzantine networked adversary; validation as the bonded act & Adversary is co-located on one machine, so consensus reduces to a daemon write, not a global commit \\
Capability-based security & Offline attenuation; authority only ever narrows & Capabilities alone as the whole story & A correctly-scoped token still permits a destructive in-scope write; bonding prices what the wall admits \\
Commons governance (Ostrom) & Boundaries, monitoring, graduated sanctions, dispute resolution & Social institutions as the enforcement medium & The four functions guide the service design (\S\ref{bonded:sec:governance}); each mechanism has its own implementation status \\
Cryptoeconomic mechanism design & Priced deterrence; market-discovered parameters & ``Code is law'' as an enforcement posture & Code is the \emph{record}, not the law: allocation stays with agents (\S\ref{bonded:sec:sen}); the daemon supplies evidence and settlement \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

